abstract: >-
  Hyperspectral Image Classification is an important topic
  in remote sensing with numerous applications in precision
  agriculture, environmental monitoring, defence, and more.
  It helps classify material composition by mapping spectral
  signatures from each pixel. Despite its potential, it
  faces persistent challenges due to the scarcity of
  labelled data and high dimensionality. This makes it
  difficult to achieve robust performance that can
  generalize well. To address this problem, we propose SRSSF
  (Stability-Reliability Spectral-Spatial Fusion), a hybrid
  mixture-of-experts framework for hyperspectral image (HSI)
  classification. It fuses a compact, stability-selected
  spectral representation with full-spectrum spatial and
  joint spectral-spatial descriptors (extended
  morphological, attribute-like, and Gabor texture
  profiles), combined through validation-derived reliability
  weights rather than fixed or heuristic fusion rules. SRSSF
  is evaluated under a strict, footprint-level spatially
  disjoint protocol in which entire patch receptive fields
  are guaranteed non-overlapping across training,
  validation, and test partitions, not mere center pixels.
  It has an automated, geometry-only (label- and
  accuracy-independent) seed-acceptance procedure and
  requires a minimum dataset-specific test-set size for
  training. The framework is benchmarked against fourteen
  classical and CNN spectral/spatial baselines and a
  dedicated ablation suite, across four benchmark datasets
  (Indian Pines, Pavia University, Salinas, and the Kennedy
  Space Center (KSC)). Our framework achieves the best OA,
  AA, and κ on every dataset, with OA scores of
  56.43,91.94,90.50, and 75.96 at Indian-Pines, Pavia
  University, Salinas, and KSC, respectively.
keywords:
  - ' hyperspectral image classification;  spatial spectral fusion;  band selection;  machine learning;  remote sensing'
